From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fleet: per-provider context levers for DeepSeek and MiniMax

Adds the `context_levers` block to each zoder profile driven by the
2026-10-25 context-optimization study. Numbers and rationale are in zoder's
docs/CONTEXT-OPTIMIZATION.md; this patch is the deployable half.

`max_context_tokens` is deliberately untouched. The levers keep the working set
small, which is what makes the existing window affordable; shrinking the window
as well is a behaviour change that should wait for the benchmark.

Not applied, not pushed: the orchestrator deploys after review.

The hunk below is a real unified diff. It was dry-run clean against
fleet/zoder-config/config.toml as of 2026-10-25; if that file has changed since,
regenerate with `diff -u` rather than forcing the patch. From the FleetOps repo root:

    git apply <path-to>/zoder-config.patch

where <path-to> is wherever this file lives (in zoder it is
gitops/fleet/zoder-config.patch). Use `git am` instead if you want the commit
message above.

---
--- a/fleet/zoder-config/config.toml
+++ b/fleet/zoder-config/config.toml
@@ -1,9 +1,49 @@
 [profiles.deepseek]
 model = "deepseek-flash"
 max_context_tokens = 128000
+# Context levers (zoder docs/CONTEXT-OPTIMIZATION.md).
+#
+# The 30 days to 2026-10-25 cost $79.79 with a 212:1 input:output ratio and
+# 98.9% of input served from cache; one CERBERUS loop spent 32.9M input tokens
+# for 110K output over ~250 calls that each resent the whole window. These
+# levers bound the working set without touching the window itself.
+#
+# compact_after_tokens = 48000: the measured loop averaged a ~28K prompt before
+# its first edit, so 48K (1.7x) never folds a legitimately large single-file
+# task but folds a 128K working set instead of resending it ~250 times.
+[profiles.deepseek.context_levers]
+tool_output_cap_bytes = 16384
+elide_after_turns = 8
+elide_min_bytes = 2048
+compact_after_tokens = 48000
+keep_recent_turns = 6
 
 [profiles.minimax]
 model = "MiniMax-M3"
 max_context_tokens = 200000
 request_timeout_s = 600
+# MiniMax publishes no cache counter, so the engine treats it as
+# ProviderCacheShape::None and asserts no hit share in either direction. Its
+# metering is by tokens and its window is 200K while a review chunk is 48K, so
+# the working set is kept tighter than DeepSeek's.
+#
+# Whether MiniMax caches prompts at all is UNVERIFIED. If it does, the
+# cache-stable prefix is a free win here too; if not, caps and compaction are
+# doing all the work. Measure before tuning these numbers.
+[profiles.minimax.context_levers]
+tool_output_cap_bytes = 12288
+elide_after_turns = 6
+elide_min_bytes = 2048
+compact_after_tokens = 32000
+keep_recent_turns = 6
+
+# Every other profile keeps the default: caps and elision on, compaction off.
+# A local vLLM model has no per-token bill, so a summarised history is pure
+# quality risk for no saving. To pin it explicitly:
+#
+# [profiles.OTHER.context_levers]
+# tool_output_cap_bytes = 16384
+# elide_after_turns = 8
+# elide_min_bytes = 2048
+# compact_after_tokens = 0        # 0 disables compaction
+# keep_recent_turns = 6
-- 
